==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|enrol}} {{qualifier|UK|Canada|Australia|New Zealand|Ireland}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|enroller}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɛnˈɹoʊl/|/ɪnˈɹoʊl/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈɹəʊl/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-enroll.ogg|Audio (Uk)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[enter]] (a name, etc.) in a [[register]], [[roll]] or [[list]]
#* {{rfdatek|en|Prescott}}
#*: All the citizen capable of bearing arms '''enrolled''' themselves.
#* {{rfdatek|en|John Milton}}
#*: An unwritten law of common right, so engraven in the hearts of our ancestors, and by them so constantly enjoyed and claimed, as that it needed not '''enrolling'''.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[enlist]] (someone) or make (someone) a [[member]] of
#: ''They were eager to '''enroll''' new recruits.''
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[enlist]] oneself (in something) or become a [[member]] (of something)
#: ''Have you '''enrolled''' in classes yet for this term?''
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[envelop]]; to [[enwrap]].
#: {{rfquotek|en|Spenser}}

====Synonyms====
